  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t)={x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}^{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}{3}{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\,{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)+3t\\end{aligned}$",
  "worked_solution": "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}{3}, &  & b(t)=-2, &  & f(t)=1, \\\\ c(t)=\\sqrt{7}, &  & d(t)=5, &  & g(t)=3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
